          "text": "{\"relation\":\"NOVEL\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Elam is primarily the literal ancient nation east of Mesopotamia and has no settled symbolic identity. Secondary readings treat it as a judged and restored Gentile nation, a military power, or an instrument of judgment against Babylon.\",\"book_summary\":\"Elam symbolizes a confederate of Babel: historically allied with Shinar, sharing Babylon’s judgment, with the breaking of Elam’s bow serving as a precursor to Babylon’s fall.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A historical nation with no fixed term-specific symbolic meaning.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Babel’s confederate, whose broken military power anticipates and participates in Babylon’s sentence.\",\"core_relation\":\"NO_CONSENSUS\",\"extension_relation\":\"NO_BASELINE\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus supplies no established symbolic class against which to set the book’s extension. The book elevates selected episodes—Elam’s alliance with Shinar and its broken bow—into a stable Babel-associated identity, rather than treating them as context-specific historical details.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction with the consensus claim that Elam was a historical military nation, but the book introduces a fixed symbolic identification absent from the consensus. It also cuts against the listed contextual reading of Elam as an instrument against Babylon by instead aligning Elam symbolically with Babel and Babylon’s judgment.\",\"rationale\":\"The consensus recognizes Elam’s military character, its bow, and its historical appearance beside Shinar, but none of the responses identifies Elam’s symbolic core as “Babel’s confederate” sharing Babylon’s sentence. Because the consensus explicitly reports no settled symbol and its alternatives do not combine these elements into the book’s identification, the entry is novel rather than a refinement of an existing consensus category.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Genesis 14 supports only a historical coalition between Elam and Shinar, while Jeremiah 49:35 and 51:56 separately describe broken bows for Elam and Babylon. The citations do not explicitly state that Elam is symbolically Babel’s confederate, that it falls under Babylon’s sentence, or that Elam’s broken bow “introduces” Babylon’s fall; those conclusions depend on typological linkage from parallel imagery.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
